{"metadata": {"team_id": "Organizers", "run_id": "orga-bm25-nopersonal", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Acid reflux occurs when the sphincter muscle situated at the lower end of the esophagus relaxes abruptly. This relaxation of the muscle at the wrong time allows stomach acid to back up to the esophagus and causes heartburn and foul breath. Constant acid reflux is a symptom of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). Some of the common factors behind acid reflux are: Medications High blood pressure Allergies Pressure on the abdomen Particular foods and drinks Hiatal hernia. It can occur suddenly at any time of the day, but the symptoms are prominent at night since people tend to relax and lay down during this period allowing the stomach acids to move into the chest. Lifestyle choices can also cause acid reflux to occur. Some causes of acid reflux include: Smoking Pregnancy Obesity Eating too late at night Consuming spicy or acidic food Having fatty foods. The symptoms of acid reflux could get worse in the third trimester but may go away after giving birth. Lying down immediately", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-33-14419:7": 8.570855140686035, "clueweb22-en0034-50-07413:1": 8.468320846557617, "clueweb22-en0009-92-03567:3": 8.125667572021484, "clueweb22-en0037-57-02718:3": 8.025704383850098, "clueweb22-en0027-99-10006:3": 8.003632545471191}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I eat dinner late at night.", "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.", "I want to change my lifestyle."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-33-14419:7": 8.570855140686035, "clueweb22-en0034-50-07413:1": 8.468320846557617, "clueweb22-en0009-92-03567:3": 8.125667572021484, "clueweb22-en0037-57-02718:3": 8.025704383850098, "clueweb22-en0027-99-10006:3": 8.003632545471191, "clueweb22-en0006-08-08635:4": 7.981046676635742, "clueweb22-en0032-81-17497:2": 7.885536193847656, "clueweb22-en0013-36-00246:1": 7.883827209472656,