{"metadata": {"team_id": "Organizers", "run_id": "orga-bm25-nopersonal", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The main difference between fractional CO2 lasers and fully ablative CO2 lasers is how much skin they remove. Fully ablative lasers completely remove the superficial skin and heat up the deeper dermal layer to trigger a regenerative healing response, which can be intense and require general anesthesia. On the other hand, fractional CO2 lasers deliver a partial beam of light that targets only a fraction of the skin, in a pixelated pattern, and are less aggressive, requiring only local anesthesia. Fractional treatments are usually better for surface-level improvement and may trigger some deeper remodeling and regeneration, but require a series of treatments for best results. Downtime can be only a matter of days, but fully ablative treatments can require up to two weeks of recovery time. Additionally, fully ablative lasers are recommended only for fair or light skin tones, as they can cause more pigmentation issues. To mitigate risks, doctors often recommend preparing the skin in advance with Retin-A or hydroquinone.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 10.039850234985352, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 7.4201226234436035, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:9": 7.39523983001709, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:11": 6.611379623413086, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:13": 6.294882297515869}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I've noticed dark spots and uneven tone on my face.", "I have visible pores on my cheeks and nose.", "I usually wear makeup to even out my complexion.", "I'm interested in treatments that improve my skin with minimal downtime."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 10.039850234985352, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 7.4201226234436035, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:9": 7.39523983001709, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:11": 6.611379623413086, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:13": 6.294882297515869, "clueweb22-en0032-14